  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Saudi duty and VAT compound, not add: 5% duty then 15% VAT = CIF × 1.2075, so a US$10,000 CIF adds US$500 duty + US$1,575 VAT = US$2,075 (20.75%). The naive 5% + 15% = 20% understates it by US$75.</li>
      <li>LCL pricing punishes small volumes: 1 CBM is US$716–$955/CBM while 10 CBM drops to US$267–$356/CBM. The high-to-high spread is US$955 ÷ US$356 = 2.68×, so the smallest shipment pays 2.68× the 10 CBM unit rate.</li>
      <li>At 10 CBM, LCL is no longer automatically cheaper: LCL totals US$2,669–$3,559 while the FCL 20GP floor starts at US$3,333. Because US$3,559 > US$3,333, the top of the LCL range already crosses the cheapest FCL quote — request both quotes from 10 CBM up.</li>
      <li>Port choice moves the clock by up to 25 days: Jeddah runs 20–35 days and Dammam/Riyadh 30–45 days, so 45 − 20 = 25 days at the extremes. The inland Dammam routing adds up to 25 days before trucking is counted.</li>
      <li>DDP air is 15–25 days faster than DDP sea: sea door-to-door is 30–35 days and air is 10–15 days, so the saving is 30 − 15 = 15 to 35 − 10 = 25 days. But the ~45 RMB/kg benchmark makes that speed expensive for heavy cargo — confirm what the DDP quote includes.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

